JOIN/S

.feature that combines rows from two or more tables based on a related column between them
.allows for efficient data retrieval by enabling the extraction of related information from multiple tables in a  single query 
.help maintain referential integrity by ensuring that relationships between tables are respected and data 
    consistency is preserved 
.help efficiently manage and organize data 
    .by allowing the use of normalized DB structures - where data is split into different tables to reduce redundancy 
.used in conjunction with GROUP BY and aggregation functions to perform comprehensive data analysis 

>>syntax
        SELECT column_names - identifies the columns to get from the tables; can be from both tables 
        FROM table1 - specifies the first table to begin joining from 
        INNER JOIN table2 - identifies we want to join both tables 
        ON table1.common_column = table2.common_column - specifies the condition on which the join is based 




Types of Joins 

Inner Join 
.returns records that have matching values in both tables 

Left join 
.returns all records from the left table and matched records from the right table 
    .if there's no match, NULL values are returned for right table columns 


Right join 
.returns all the rows from the right table and the matched rows from the left table 
    NULL values will be returned for columns from the left table when there are no matches 


Full join 
.returns all records when there is a match in either the left or right table 
    .if no match, NULL values are returned for columns that have no match in either table 
>> its a left-join result set combined with a right-join result set to retrieve all employee/department names - for the sample tables I used 


Cross Join 
.returns the Cartesian product of two tables 
    .matches every row of one table with every row of another table - all possible combinations between the employees and departments rows 



Self Join 
.a table is joined to itself 
.useful when there's need to compare rows with the same table
    e.g relating employees to their managers in an organizational hierarchy

